---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'SERVICE', title: 'Service pages', text: 'A separate page for every service you offer. It answers the searcher’s questions and leads them to a call or a booking.' },
  { label: 'DISTRICT', title: 'District and city', text: 'Opened only when there’s something real to say about that area. We don’t write copies with the city name swapped.' },
  { label: 'GUIDE', title: 'Guide articles', text: 'They catch people looking for information early and send them to the service page. We don’t move on to guides before the service pages are done.' },
  { label: 'APPROVAL', title: 'Expert approval', text: 'The draft comes from us, the sign-off on accuracy from you. On legal and health sites we write to that profession’s advertising rules.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'nedir', label: 'What does content production cover?' },
  { id: 'sira', label: 'Why service pages first?' },
  { id: 'sehir', label: 'District and city pages' },
  { id: 'kurallar', label: 'Legal and health sites' },
  { id: 'surec', label: 'How a page gets written' },
  { id: 'yapay-zeka', label: 'Use of AI' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Who writes the content?',
    a: 'We do the research and the draft, and you approve the accuracy of the professional information. On legal and health sites no page goes live without expert approval.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How many pieces of content do I need per month?',
    a: 'We don’t give a fixed number. The missing service pages get finished first, and that list of gaps sets the number. After that we choose every page to write by the real demand in searches.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does blogging affect my place on the map?',
    a: 'Its direct effect is limited. A blog brings people looking for information to the site and carries them to your service page through links. For your place on the map, your Google Business Profile and your service pages matter more.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What happens to my existing articles?',
    a: 'We look at them first. Articles that get impressions but no clicks, or that compete with another of your pages for the same search, we rewrite or merge. We recommend removing articles that get no impressions at all and have little to do with your business.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="services/content"
  title="SEO Content Production | Wiserrise"
  description="SEO content for local business sites: service pages first, then district pages and guides built on real information. Published with expert approval."
  eyebrow="SERVICE · CONTENT PRODUCTION"
  h1="Complete your missing pages with SEO content production."
  crumb="Content production"
  lede="Content production is the work of writing the pages missing from your site. First we complete the service pages that bring customers to the phone; the blog comes last. We work out which pages are missing from search data and from the services on your Google profile."
  serviceName="SEO Content Production"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Services', href: p('services') },
    { label: 'SEO', href: p('services/seo') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'SEO', href: p('services/seo'), text: 'Groundwork, page structure, content and links: the main service this one belongs to.' },
    { label: 'Local SEO', href: p('services/local-seo'), text: 'We fix your profile and your site together and work on getting you to stand out on the map.' },
    { label: 'On-Page SEO', href: p('services/on-page-seo'), text: 'We fit each page to a single search and fix titles and internal links.' },
    { label: 'Technical SEO', href: p('services/technical-seo'), text: 'We fix crawling and indexing errors so the pages we write reach Google.' },
  ]}
  ctaLabel="Get a free analysis"
  ctaHref="/#contact"
>
  <h2 id="nedir">What does content production cover?</h2>

  <p>
    Every page that should be on your site and isn’t. For a local business, service pages head the list and blog articles
    come last.
  </p>

  <p>
    When someone in İzmir searches “Bornova vehicle inspection”, Google looks for a page that describes that service in that
    area. If your site has only a home page and a contact page, Google has nothing to show.
  </p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Page</th>
          <th>Example search</th>
          <th>What it does</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Service page</strong></td>
          <td>“root canal treatment”, “divorce lawyer”</td>
          <td>Meets the person who is ready to buy. The page closest to a phone call.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>District or city page</strong></td>
          <td>“Bornova vehicle inspection”</td>
          <td>Gets you seen in the area you serve. Real local information is a must.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Guide article</strong></td>
          <td>“how many sessions does a root canal take”</td>
          <td>Catches people looking for information early and carries them to the service page through a link.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="sira">Why service pages first?</h2>

  <p>
    Because they’re what bring in customers. If you draw visitors with guide articles to a site with no service pages, they
    find nowhere to go and leave.
  </p>

  <p>
    That’s why we don’t write blog posts until the service pages are done. When a business asks for a blog, we show them the
    list of missing service pages first.
  </p>

  <p>
    We build the list from searches in Search Console that get impressions but have no matching page, and from pages your
    competitors have and you don’t. The fastest source is your <a href={p('services/gbp')}>Google Business Profile</a>:
    every service listed on the profile with no page on the site goes on the list.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sehir">When do you open a district or city page?</h2>

  <p>
    When you have something real to say about that area. Google treats copied pages with only the city name changed as
    “doorway” pages, and they harm the whole site. We explain the rules for branches and for the cities you serve on the
    <a href={`${p('services/local-seo')}#coklu`}>local SEO page</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    What does something real mean? Say you have a physiotherapy center in Kadıköy and also do home visits in Ataşehir. An
    Ataşehir page could cover:
  </p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>Which neighborhoods in Ataşehir you go to</li>
    <li>Which treatments can be done on a home visit</li>
    <li>How home visits are scheduled, and how to get to the center for those who want to come in</li>
    <li>The questions patients from that area ask most on the phone</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    If we don’t have this information, we don’t open the page. Dropping a district name into a template and multiplying it
    looks fast, and it weakens the site in Google’s eyes.
  </p>

  <p>
    The <a href={p('services/rank-tracking')}>measurement</a> we take across your area decides which district gets a page
    first. Districts where you really get work but lag behind in search move to the front of the line.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kurallar">What do we watch for on legal and health sites?</h2>

  <p>
    Professional rules. On lawyers’ sites, the Turkish Bar Association’s advertising regulation (TBB Reklam Yasağı
    Yönetmeliği) applies. Promises of winning, success rates, superiority claims like “the best” and client testimonials are
    off limits.
  </p>

  <p>
    Health has similar limits. We don’t write treatment guarantees or before-and-after promises. Every text goes through
    this filter before publication and gets the lawyer’s or the doctor’s approval.
  </p>

  <p>
    In these industries, informative copy written without claims also comes across as more trustworthy to readers. The
    industry-specific details are on the <a href={p('industries/law')}>SEO for lawyers</a> and
    <a href={p('industries/dental')}>SEO for dentists</a> pages.
  </p>

  <h2 id="surec">How does a page get written?</h2>

  <p>Every page goes through the same steps:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Gap list.</strong> From the sources above, we list the pages missing from your site.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Order.</strong> The page closest to a phone call goes first; guide articles come last.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Plan.</strong> For each page we set the target search, the questions to answer, the internal links to add and
      the schema type. This part belongs to the
      <a href={p('services/on-page-seo')} style="white-space: nowrap;">on-page SEO</a> work.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Writing and approval.</strong> We write the draft, and you approve the accuracy of the details about your
      business.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Publishing.</strong> We put the page on the site, link it in and add it to the sitemap. If we can’t manage the
      whole site, we don’t start, and that’s why publishing stays with us.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Follow-up.</strong> We track which searches the page appears in through Search Console and add the questions
      that are still missing.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <p>
    You see what we did and how the measurement changed in your weekly report. We can’t give you a timeframe for search;
    for a page to reach Google, the <a href={p('services/technical-seo')}>technical SEO</a> side has to be
    clean first.
  </p>

  <h2 id="yapay-zeka">Do you use AI?</h2>

  <p>
    We do, for research and the first draft. We don’t publish the raw output as it is.
  </p>

  <p>
    AI doesn’t know your business. Copy written without knowing which districts you go to and what customers ask on the
    phone doesn’t stand apart from similar pages targeting the same search. And a page that doesn’t stand apart doesn’t
    rank.
  </p>

  <p>
    Getting mentioned in AI answers depends on the same thing. ChatGPT and Gemini lean on sources that answer the question
    clearly and describe the business the same way everywhere; we measure this side in our <a href={p('services/geo')}>GEO</a>
    service.
  </p>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2>Which pages are <span class="highlight">missing</span> from your site?</h2>
    <p>
      The free analysis is a video we record for your business: where you stand today and what to start with first. It
      lands in your inbox within <span style="white-space: nowrap;">24 hours</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
